  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t))\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81-\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t,-3\\operatorname{sin}\u20613t\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0 \\\\ \u27e81-\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t,-3\\operatorname{sin}\u20613t\u27e9 & =\u27e80,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t=0\\, & \u21d2\\,t=0, \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}\u20613t=0\\, & \u21d2\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the object is stationary.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
